{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013UfW6fV83qyp27vtpvShfh","type":"tool_result","content":"=== V1 L1258-1266 ===\n\n---\n\n**Q13.** *[retrieval: ch17]* A request crosses six microservices. How do the spans emitted by service six come to be associated with the trace begun at service one?\n\n- A) The tracing backend correlates them by comparing timestamps after ingestion\n- B) Each service includes a trace ID and span ID in the context passed to the next, which uses those values to create a span belonging to the same trace\n- C) Each service queries a central trace registry for the active trace\n- D) The service mesh control plane assigns trace IDs from a shared pool\n\n=== V2 Q12/Q13 ===\n\n- A) The tracing backend correlates them by comparing timestamps after ingestion\n- B) Each service includes a trace ID and span ID in the context passed to the next, which uses those values to create a span belonging to the same trace\n- C) Each service queries a central trace registry for the active trace\n- D) The service mesh control plane assigns trace IDs from a shared pool\n\n---\n\n**Q14.** *[retrieval: ch6]* A logging agent must run on every node, including nodes added after deployment. Which workload resource is correct?","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eedca767-6404-4917-ae73-7379181f9d4c","uuid":"f473ddd2-8293-4577-8701-124da48b49ff","timestamp":"2026-08-31T21:57:03.462Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== V1 L1258-1266 ===\n\n---\n\n**Q13.** *[retrieval: ch17]* A request crosses six microservices. How do the spans emitted by service six come to be associated with the trace begun at service one?\n\n- A) The tracing backend correlates them by comparing timestamps after ingestion\n- B) Each service includes a trace ID and span ID in the context passed to the next, which uses those values to create a span belonging to the same trace\n- C) Each service queries a central trace registry for the active trace\n- D) The service mesh control plane assigns trace IDs from a shared pool\n\n=== V2 Q12/Q13 ===\n\n- A) The tracing backend correlates them by comparing timestamps after ingestion\n- B) Each service includes a trace ID and span ID in the context passed to the next, which uses those values to create a span belonging to the same trace\n- C) Each service queries a central trace registry for the active trace\n- D) The service mesh control plane assigns trace IDs from a shared pool\n\n---\n\n**Q14.** *[retrieval: ch6]* A logging agent must run on every node, including nodes added after deployment. Which workload resource is correct?","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0152ZqnHjvY3mp9ByUJLMZpN","type":"tool_result","content":"19:- Mis-tagged retrieval items (WARN): **1**\n128:(limit-as-denominator, node-as-denominator, admission-time defaulting). Both tagged `[retrieval: ch5]`.\n147:spaced-retrieval structure, and the format change does real work. No fix required if budget is tight.\n158:**Issue:** **mis-tagged as `[retrieval: ch17]`.** Nothing from Chapter 17 is tested. The correct\n160:microservices\" is scenery, not a retrieval target — a reader with no memory of Ch 17 answers this at\n166:**Effect on the count:** practice-section retrieval drops from 4/17 (23.5%) to **3/17 (17.6%)** if\n299:- Actual, checkpoints: **26.7%** (4 of 15 tagged `[retrieval: chN]`) —\n328:source chapters (5, 6, 10, 13) qualify, and Ch 10 is eight chapters back. No two retrieval items in\n331:**Uncounted strength.** All **8** Soundings questions are retrieval by construction — every one sources\n333:the checkpoint percentage by design, but the chapter's true spaced-retrieval load is 16 of 40 items.\n336:replace its retrieval tag by tagging the incoming §4 component item `[retrieval: ch13]` and framing it\n419:`[retrieval: ch13]` to preserve 4/15 spacing and deliver the metrics-server × Prometheus join the\n434:naming the TAG whitepaper's five (prepares P3's best distractor); P13 retrieval tag dropped or made\n438:**Verdict: WARN, no gate-blocking FAIL.** Why-wrong coverage is complete across all 20 multiple-choice items, budget is met exactly in all four categories, the Soundings spoiler check is clean against all six Fixed Points, and retrieval spacing is at the intended ceiling drawing from five chapters. One item (CP1 Q4) is ambiguous and should be retired; the factual question underneath it — whether Kubernetes copies a limit into an unset request — belongs to the fact-accuracy audit, and I've routed it there rather than ruling on it. The one structural finding worth the auth